00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.720
Hello, welcome to the AI Coffee Break!&nbsp;
Today, Ms. Coffee Bean has prepared the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.080
definitive introduction to Graph&nbsp;
Neural Networks! Or GNNs in short.&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:29.120
But Ms. Coffee Bean, are you not a little&nbsp;
over-confident to say "definitive"? Phsssht!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:36.960
I just wonder if… psshht! Ok, ehm, here we go:
I always struggled with Graph Neural Networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.880
in machine learning classes. But one day, I could&nbsp;
not get around them anymore and had to learn them!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.480
They are such a huge topic in machine learning for&nbsp;
a very obvious reason: graphs are everywhere! And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:57.120
not just here artistically represented in this&nbsp;
stock image. Let's take for example air travel.&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.000
Flights from origin to destination&nbsp;
with perhaps a stop in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.640
can be represented as a graph, where airlines try&nbsp;
to maximize the number of passengers transported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:15.600
by trying to minimize fuel, time and other costs.
Navigation systems on smaller scales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.920
on street maps, also do graph optimization&nbsp;
for getting you to your destination.&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:27.360
Other examples of graphs are personal connections&nbsp;
or graphs that social media apps live of.&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:34.880
Or even text is also a graph if we think of it not&nbsp;
as a sequence, but if we analyze it linguistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:42.800
in terms of the dependency parse, for example.
Images are graphs too in multiple senses: We make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.280
one kind of graph interpretation of images when&nbsp;
we represent each pixel as a graph node and each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:56.560
pixel is connected with an edge to its neighbors.&nbsp;
This is a very regular kind of graph, a grid.&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:04.640
Another, more semantic graph interpretation of&nbsp;
an image, can be in terms of the scene graph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.360
where objects and entities are nodes,&nbsp;
connected by their relation in the image.&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.160
One more example and we get finally to Graph&nbsp;
Neural Networks: In NLP people are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.840
in the knowledge graphs comprising facts&nbsp;
about the world, like these true facts here.&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.200
Well, I hope you are now convinced&nbsp;
now how ubiquitous graphs are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.280
so of course we need a deep neural model to&nbsp;
handle these structures, right? Well, graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:39.040
neural networks are the deep and neural solution&nbsp;
to this. They come in many shapes and flavors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.960
but we want here to stay simple and explain&nbsp;
only the Graph Convolutional Neural Networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.160
If you understand this one, you will also&nbsp;
understand the basics of the others architectures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:56.240
because they work based on the same principles.
And Graph Convolutional Neural Nets are not hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:04.000
you just have to apply this formula iteratively&nbsp;
and you are done. Wait what? Well if that’s it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.560
let’s break it down to every detail:
First, let’s talk about the nodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:16.080
They are denoted here with h, where h is a vector&nbsp;
representation of the nodes. These vectors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.760
chosen depending on the application: They can be&nbsp;
word vectors when working with knowledge bases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.520
they can be pixel values when working with images.&nbsp;
They can be a combination of image features and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.960
word vectors if working with scene graphs!
In Graph Neural Networks, the idea is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.880
we have initial vector representations for our&nbsp;
problem. But we can do even better, if we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.640
the neighbors and relations into account. So&nbsp;
in principle, we update our initial vectors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:51.120
using information in the graph structure and&nbsp;
get vectors that represent the problem better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:58.160
So the key is not in the initial representation&nbsp;
of the nodes, but in how one aggregates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.880
representations of the neighbors in order to get&nbsp;
an even better representation than the original.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.680
This is why we apply this formula recursively, to&nbsp;
get another, better vector h at each time step.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.560
Only the question now, is how do we&nbsp;
exactly get this better representation?&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.040
First, when computing the new representation,&nbsp;
we want to keep something from the old one. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.640
example when working with a knowledge base that&nbsp;
truthfully says that Ms. Coffee Bean is a star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.960
when computing a new vector for her, now knowing&nbsp;
that she is a star, we still want to keep the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.320
knowledge of her identity. This is why, we&nbsp;
take the old representation and multiply it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:44.720
by a weight matrix, deciding how this previous&nbsp;
knowledge should be transformed. But how do we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.840
know this weight matrix? Well, we don’t! We let&nbsp;
a neural network decide for it! We train it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.200
our given data of node labels, edge labels or&nbsp;
other predictions from the graph structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.720
When training with these objectives, the neural&nbsp;
network learns to make right predictions given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.720
the new representations, so it better should&nbsp;
learn the weight matrix that helps to get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:15.760
new representation. This is how we decide on what&nbsp;
and how to keep from our original representation.&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.640
Now, we also want to aggregate the&nbsp;
information from the neighbors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.000
So we take their representation and also let a&nbsp;
neural network decide how to transform it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.440
what to keep from it for being able to solve the&nbsp;
given task, for example node label prediction.&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.480
And here is where the magic of the graph&nbsp;
convolutional neural network happens:&nbsp;

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.960
In the aggregation function.&nbsp;

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.560
By this sum we say that we sum over all&nbsp;
transformed neighbor representations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.880
By c_ij we mean that we normalize the&nbsp;
vectors differently for each neighbor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.880
But this is not really important, because crucial&nbsp;
here, is that the normalizing sum here is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:04.000
permutation-invariant aggregation function.&nbsp;
A whaat now? Well, this means that however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.320
we aggregate the information from the neighbors,&nbsp;
the way we do this has to be insensitive to order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.680
Because there is no intrinsic ordering to a&nbsp;
graph, how would we ever decide if we take node 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:24.000
or node 2 first? So we don’t decide, because we&nbsp;
take a permutation-invariant function, delivering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:30.000
the same result given any order. Another&nbsp;
example for a permutation-invariant function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:36.080
would be the product or the maximum function,&nbsp;
because they do not depend on argument order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.560
And because we aggregate the information from&nbsp;
the nodes, similarly to how we do in CNNs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.640
(convolutional neural networks), this is then&nbsp;
called the convolutional graph neural network.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:55.200
Of course, the name "convolution" is misleading,&nbsp;
because the convolution in images is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:01.280
permutation-invariant (you can check it), but&nbsp;
still, it is a valid operation if we regard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:08.320
images as graphs, because images are a special&nbsp;
type of graphs, where the order is given. So yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.640
the name convolution is misleading, but it is how&nbsp;
it is! And here we are: If we apply this formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.800
iteratively, at each timestep we learn a better&nbsp;
graph representation comprising node information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:27.280
also from neighbors and their neighbors.
These new informed and refined representations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.800
should be then suitable to be used for the&nbsp;
new task of, for example link prediction, node&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:40.880
classification or even entire graph classification&nbsp;
and many other tasks. GNNs have been successfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.200
applied in the medical domain for example, to&nbsp;
predict side-effects due to drug interactions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.840
Or in numerous graph optimization problems,&nbsp;
like the travelling salesman problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.880
Or for various applications in knowledge&nbsp;
graph prediction. They can be even used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:03.520
predicting object trajectories from video. These&nbsp;
applications are just a few from a very big list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.360
Where there is a graph (so everywhere), there can&nbsp;
be a graph neural network solving the problem.&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:14.480
So this was it, we hope that now you have&nbsp;
a better understanding of Graph Neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.520
Networks and are curious enough to go out&nbsp;
and explore the topic of GNNs by yourself!&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:25.840
Hey, do not forget to like and subscribe!
See you!

